        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Feudalism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 800 CE – 1789 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Power held by</span><span class="info-stat-val">A king or prince at the head of a hierarchy of lords and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, each lord exercising justice and command over the land and people granted to him</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Legitimacy from</span><span class="info-stat-val">Oaths of homage and fealty, the grant of the fief, custom, and the Church’s teaching that society was ordered into those who pray, those who fight and those who work</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How rulers are chosen</span><span class="info-stat-val">Kingdoms and fiefs passed by inheritance; each heir did homage to his lord and paid a relief before taking possession, and a disloyal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> could forfeit his fief</span></div>
