    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Feudalism bound lords and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> by oaths of homage and fealty: the lord granted a fief, usually land, and the vassal owed military service, counsel and loyalty in return.</li><li>It grew as Carolingian royal power broke up in the 9th and 10th centuries, gave castle lords local power over peasants, and was abolished in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on the night of 4 August 1789.</li><li>Since Elizabeth A. R. Brown’s critique of 1974 and Susan Reynolds’s Fiefs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">Vassals</a> of 1994, historians have debated whether the term fits medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, let alone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Sark in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/channel-islands">Channel Islands</a>, settled in 1565 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helier-de-carteret">Helier de Carteret</a> under a charter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> and divided into forty tenements whose holders sat in its parliament, kept what was called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>’s last feudal system until 2008.</p></aside>
    </section>

