    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 800</span><span class="rf-text">Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, grants of land to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> in return for service become the usual reward of royal followers</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1000</span><span class="rf-text">Castles multiply in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as castellans seize rights of justice and taxation over the peasants around them</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1086</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> has the landholders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> swear fealty to him at Salisbury</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1192</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a> becomes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, ruling through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> often compared with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>’s</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1215</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> accepts <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta">Magna Carta</a>, which limits the feudal dues a king may demand of his barons</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1337</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-vi-of-france">Philip VI of France</a> confiscates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> from his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a>; the Hundred Years’ War begins</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1789</span><span class="rf-text">On the night of 4 August the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> votes to abolish the feudal regime</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="works">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Works</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vassal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vassal.jpg" alt="Vassal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vassal">Vassal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayeux_Tapestry_scene23_Harold_sacramentum_fecit_Willelmo_duci.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Myrabella, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the narrow sense defined by the Belgian historian François-Louis Ganshof, feudalism was a set of mutual obligations within the warrior nobility, built on three elements: the lord, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> and the fief. A man became a vassal through commendation: he did homage, placing his joined hands between his lord’s and declaring himself the lord’s man, and then swore fealty, an oath of fidelity, often on relics. The lord owed protection and maintenance, usually through a fief, most often land with the peasants who worked it but sometimes an office, a toll or a money rent. The vassal owed aid and counsel: military service, limited in 12th-century northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to forty days a year on offensive campaigns, guard duty in the lord’s castle, attendance at his court to advise him and help judge disputes, and payments on set occasions.</p><p>By the middle of the 10th century fiefs had generally become hereditary, but an heir had to do homage afresh and pay a relief, and the lord kept rights that recalled his ultimate ownership: wardship of under-age heirs, the marriage of heiresses, aids to ransom him, knight his eldest son or marry his eldest daughter, and escheat, the return of the fief when a line died out. Vassals granted parts of their fiefs to vassals of their own, and many held land from several lords, which led in the 12th century to liege homage, a bond owed above all others. Beneath this structure lay the manor. In Marc Bloch’s broader picture of feudal society, peasants, many of them serfs bound to the soil, worked the lord’s demesne, paid dues in kind or money, ground their grain at his mill and answered to his court.</p><p>Feudal government divided public power among private persons. Rights of justice, taxation and command that had belonged to kings passed to counts, castellans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and abbots, so that a king governed directly only his own domain and dealt with the rest of his kingdom through his great vassals. The limits on rulers were those of contract and custom: a lord who broke faith could be defied, and vassals expected to be consulted on war, law and extraordinary taxation. This distinguishes feudal monarchy from the absolute monarchies that later replaced it, which governed through salaried officials, standing armies and regular taxes, and feudalism itself from manorialism, the economic order of estates worked by dependent peasants, which was older than feudal bonds and outlasted them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-martel.jpg" alt="Charles Martel" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Martel_divise_le_royaume_entre_P%C3%A9pin_et_Carloman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Feudal bonds grew from older Roman and Frankish practices. Late Roman landlords bound their tenants, the coloni, to the soil, and powerful men surrounded themselves with armed followers; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> added the personal bond of commendation and the grant of land for life, the beneficium or benefice. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a>, the Frankish mayor of the palace who died in 741, was the first to reward his followers with lands on a large scale, much of it taken from the Church, and the German historian Heinrich Brunner argued in 1887 that he did so to pay for a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>; Lynn White Jr. tied this in 1962 to the arrival of the stirrup in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Most historians now reject both theses as too simple. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> (768–814) <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> and the grant of land became firmly linked, and royal vassals held benefices throughout the empire.</p><p>The system took shape as Carolingian power broke up. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> divided the empire in 843, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> and Saracen raids exposed the weakness of distant kings, and defence passed to counts and local lords who could raise mounted troops. In 847 Charlemagne’s grandsons, meeting at Meerssen, declared that every free man should choose a lord, the king or one of his men, and counties and benefices became hereditary in practice. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> word feudum began to replace beneficium in charters of the late 9th and 10th centuries. In 1953 Georges Duby, studying the Mâconnais in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, argued that around 1000 the counts’ public courts gave way to castellans who ruled the surrounding peasants by force, a “feudal revolution” that later historians have both extended and disputed.</p><p>No one in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> spoke of feudalism. Feudum probably comes from a Frankish word for movable wealth related to the Germanic fehu, “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>”, though other derivations, from Latin and even Arabic, have been proposed. Seventeenth-century lawyers wrote of feudal law, and the idea of a feudal regime took hold in the 18th century through Henri de Boulainvilliers, who saw in it the ancient liberties of the Frankish nobility, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a>, who devoted the last books of The Spirit of the Laws (1748) to the feudal laws of the Franks. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a> wrote of feudal government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-wealth-of-nations">The Wealth of Nations</a> (1776), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> writers used the word to attack the privileges of their own day, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> made feudalism the stage of history before capitalism, defined by lords’ control of land and their exploitation of the peasants who worked it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="history">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Through History</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror.jpg" alt="William the Conqueror" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_the_Conqueror_(TFA).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Myrabella, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Feudal institutions spread with Frankish and Norman lords. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> of 1066 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> treated all English land as held of himself and granted much of it to his barons and churches in return for set quotas of knights, and in 1086 he had the landholders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> swear fealty to him at Salisbury, binding even the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of his vassals to the crown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> carried feudal custom to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>, where the jurist John of Ibelin wrote the most elaborate treatise of feudal law in the 1260s, and to the Greek lands divided into fiefs after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> of 1204. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> lawyers compiled the Libri Feudorum, the law of fiefs studied in the universities, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sachsenspiegel">Sachsenspiegel</a> of about 1230 ranked the princes and nobles of the empire in seven levels of the army shield.</p><p>Kings learned to make feudal law an instrument of their own power. In 1202 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a> had his court declare the French fiefs of his vassal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of England forfeit, and in 1204 he conquered Normandy. In England, John’s arbitrary reliefs and other feudal demands drove his barons to impose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta">Magna Carta</a> in 1215, which fixed the relief for an earl’s or a baron’s barony at £100 and required the common counsel of the kingdom for any scutage or aid beyond the three customary ones. The bond also carried old dangers: in 1337 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-vi-of-france">Philip VI of France</a> confiscated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> from his vassal Edward III, who was also King of England, and the Hundred Years’ War began. Outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, historians have drawn the closest parallel with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> in 1192 and rewarded his vassals, the gokenin, with offices and rights over land.</p><p>From the 14th century the military side of feudalism declined. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> of 1347–1351 cut the labour force and weakened lords’ hold over peasants, armies came to rely on paid troops, and English historians speak of “bastard feudalism”, in which lords retained followers by contracts and fees rather than land. East of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> lords tied peasants more tightly to the land in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-serfdom">second serfdom</a> from the 16th century, and in Japan the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> ruled from 1603 to 1868 through some 260 domain lords, whose domains were abolished in 1871. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> seigneurial dues and courts survived until the night of 4 August 1789, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a>, alarmed by peasant risings, declared that it “abolishes the feudal system entirely”.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="strengths">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Strengths</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cortes-of-leon-of-1188"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cortes-of-leon-of-1188.jpg" alt="Cortes of León of 1188" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cortes-of-leon-of-1188">Cortes of León of 1188</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bas%C3%ADlica_de_San_Isidoro_de_Le%C3%B3n._Claustro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">José Luis Filpo Cabana, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Historians have long credited feudal ties with holding society together when larger structures failed. Marc Bloch opened Feudal Society (1939–1940) with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> and Saracen invasions and presented the bonds of lordship and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> as the means by which a world without effective kings organised its own protection. Feudal tenure also solved a practical problem: a ruler without a bureaucracy or much coined money could maintain armoured cavalry by granting land instead of pay. Joseph Strayer, who defined feudalism by fragmented political authority, public power in private hands and armies raised through private contracts, argued in On the Medieval Origins of the Modern State (1970) that the feudal monarchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where kings built on the obligations of their vassals, became the cradles of the modern European state.</p><p>Defenders of the feudal tradition stressed its reciprocity. The bond obliged the lord as well as the vassal, and feudal custom allowed a vassal whose lord failed him to renounce his homage. The vassal’s duty of counsel grew into the great assemblies from which parliaments developed: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes-of-leon-of-1188">Cortes of León of 1188</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> recognises as the oldest documentary evidence of the European parliamentary system, and the English Parliament to which Simon de Montfort summoned knights of the shires and burgesses in 1265. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a> traced England’s free constitution back to the customs of the ancient Germans, and opponents of absolute monarchy in the 17th and 18th centuries invoked feudal custom against royal claims to unlimited power.</p><p>The order also rested on custom that bound the strong as well as the weak. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/villein">Villeins</a> were not slaves: they held their land by inheritance in practice and had recourse to the manorial court under its custom, and from the 13th century their labour services were increasingly turned into fixed money payments. Comparative historians have found further merits. Edwin O. Reischauer argued in the 1950s that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, which alone had passed through feudalism, were the first societies to modernise, and suggested that the divided power and contractual loyalties of their feudal past had helped.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="weaknesses">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Weaknesses</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adam-smith.jpg" alt="Adam Smith" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adam_Smith_The_Muir_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Critics, from medieval churchmen to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> economists, saw in feudalism a recipe for disorder. Where castellans took over public rights, private war became endemic, and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-charroux">Council of Charroux</a> in 989 the Peace of God movement tried to shield clergy, peasants and their goods from the violence of knights. During the civil war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> known as the Anarchy (1138–1153) barons built castles and fought for rival claimants, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peterborough">Peterborough</a> Chronicle reported men saying openly that Christ and his saints slept. Multiple <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> tangled loyalties: as dukes of Aquitaine the kings of England were vassals of the French crown, a source of repeated war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a> judged that under feudal law the authority of government remained “too weak in the head and too strong in the inferior members”.</p><p>The peasants bore its costs. Serfs owed labour on the lord’s demesne, could not leave the manor or marry outside it without his leave, and paid to use his mill and oven and to have their cases heard in his court. Revolts showed the anger this caused: the Jacquerie of 1358 in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the English Peasants’ Revolt of 1381, whose preacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ball">John Ball</a> asked, as the chronicler Thomas Walsingham reported, “When Adam delved and Eve span, who was then the gentleman?” For <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> and later Marxist historians the system was above all a means by which a landed class extracted labour, produce and rent from those who worked the land, and in France seigneurial dues and courts still burdened the peasantry in 1789.</p><p>As a way of raising armies feudal service was unreliable. It was limited in time and place, vassals disputed whether they owed service abroad, and in 1297 Roger Bigod, Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a>, refused Edward I’s order to lead troops to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> unless the king went in person. Kings came to prefer paid soldiers and taxes granted by assemblies, and the infantry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> of the 14th and 15th centuries reduced the value of the mounted knight. The absolute monarchies of the 16th to 18th centuries kept the nobility’s privileges while removing its independent power, and Enlightenment writers attacked the surviving seigneurial rights as irrational relics that burdened peasants and hindered trade.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Impact &amp; Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/joseph-bonaparte"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-bonaparte.jpg" alt="Joseph Bonaparte" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/joseph-bonaparte">Joseph Bonaparte</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph-Bonaparte.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">François Gérard, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The abolition of 1789 was followed across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. French armies and their allies carried it into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-bonaparte">Joseph Bonaparte</a> abolished feudalism in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in 1806 and the Sicilian parliament followed in 1812; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> began freeing its serfs in 1807, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> abolished peasants’ labour dues in 1848 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> emancipated its serfs in 1861. Remnants lasted far longer: the last seigneurial rents in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> were paid in 1970, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> ended feudal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-tenure">land tenure</a> only in 2004, and Sark replaced the tenants’ seats in its parliament, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chief-pleas">Chief Pleas</a>, with elected members in 2008. Guernsey still has fiefs whose seigneurs attend its Court of Chief Pleas, and English land law still speaks of estates held in fee simple, a descendant of the fief.</p><p>The feudal past left a double mark on European political thought. Ideas of contract, counsel and consent fed the parliaments of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and the arguments of opponents of absolute monarchy, while from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> on “feudal” became a byword for privilege and backwardness. Comparative historians have applied the term to many societies: to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunates</a>; to Zhou <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, whose system of enfeoffment, fengjian, gave modern Chinese its word for feudal; to medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>; and to grants of revenue in return for service such as the Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pronoia">pronoia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a> of the Islamic world and the Mughal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a>. Rushton Coulborn’s Feudalism in History (1956) set such cases side by side.</p><p>Historians now argue over the word itself. In The Tyranny of a Construct (1974) Elizabeth A. R. Brown argued that feudalism was a construct of later scholars that imposed a false uniformity on the varied ties between medieval lords and their dependants, and in Fiefs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">Vassals</a> (1994) Susan Reynolds showed that the classic model of fiefs and vassalage owed much to the academic lawyers of the 12th century and after, and that earlier documents do not bear it out. Others reply that the word remains useful shorthand if carefully defined, in Ganshof’s narrow legal sense or Bloch’s broad social one, and economic historians keep it for the relation between lords and dependent peasants. Specialists on Japan such as Karl Friday now stress differences from Europe rather than similarities, and many textbooks avoid the term altogether.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Feudalism names the medieval order in which lords and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> exchanged land for service and loyalty and public power passed into private hands. Born of the Carolingian collapse, it shaped European kingship, law and parliaments until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> abolished it in 1789, and historians still debate whether the term fits medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> or anywhere else.</em></p>

